::ALG2_U03_Pretest_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_Pretest_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.7; MLA.A2.AE.8<br><br>Question:<br>Which expression is equivalent to &radic;72? {
= 6&radic;2 #Correct. Factor 72 as 36 times 2, then &radic;36 = 6, so &radic;72 = 6&radic;2.
~ 8&radic;9 #This does not simplify the radical correctly because &radic;72 is not the same as 8&radic;9.
~ 36&radic;2 #This uses 36 as a factor but does not take its square root.
~ 12&radic;6 #This product is too large because 12&radic;6 squared would not equal 72.
}

::ALG2_U03_Pretest_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_Pretest_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.7; MLA.A2.AE.8<br><br>Question:<br>What is the value of 16<sup>3/4</sup>? {
~ 4 #This uses only the fourth root of 16 and does not apply the power of 3.
~ 6 #This is not produced by the rational exponent relationship.
= 8 #Correct. The fourth root of 16 is 2, and 2<sup>3</sup> = 8.
~ 12 #This treats the exponent operation like multiplication by 3 after taking a root.
}

::ALG2_U03_Pretest_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_Pretest_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.RR.2<br><br>Question:<br>What is the domain of f(x) = &radic;(x - 5)? {
~ x <= 5 #This reverses the inequality. The radicand must be nonnegative.
= x >= 5 #Correct. Set x - 5 >= 0, so x >= 5.
~ all real numbers #Square root functions can have restricted domains when the radicand includes a variable expression.
~ x &ne; 5 #This is a denominator-style restriction, not a square root domain condition.
}

::ALG2_U03_Pretest_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_Pretest_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.RR.1; MLA.A2.RR.3<br><br>Question:<br>Solve &radic;(x + 7) = 4. {
~ x = -3 #This comes from subtracting incorrectly after squaring.
~ x = 4 #This repeats the square root value instead of solving for x.
~ x = 16 #This does not account for the +7 inside the radical.
= x = 9 #Correct. Squaring gives x + 7 = 16, so x = 9, and the check works.
}

::ALG2_U03_Pretest_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_Pretest_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.6<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify (x<sup>2</sup> - 9)/(x - 3). {
= x + 3, x &ne; 3 #Correct. Factor the numerator as (x - 3)(x + 3), cancel the common factor, and keep the original restriction x &ne; 3.
~ x - 3, x &ne; -3 #This cancels the wrong factor and gives the wrong restriction.
~ x + 3, all real x #The simplified expression hides the original restriction, but x = 3 is still not allowed.
~ x<sup>2</sup> + 3, x &ne; 3 #This does not factor and simplify the rational expression correctly.
}

::ALG2_U03_Pretest_Q06::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_Pretest_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only extension of MLA.A2.AE.6; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>What is 1/x + 2/x, with x &ne; 0? {
~ 2/x<sup>2</sup> #This multiplies the numerators and denominators instead of adding like rational terms.
= 3/x #Correct. The denominators already match, so add the numerators: 1 + 2 = 3.
~ 3/(2x) #This changes the denominator even though the fractions already have a common denominator.
~ x/3 #This flips the expression and does not preserve the sum.
}

::ALG2_U03_Pretest_Q07::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_Pretest_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.RR.4<br><br>Question:<br>Solve 2/x = 5, with x &ne; 0. {
~ x = 5/2 #This is the reciprocal of the correct solution.
~ x = 10 #This comes from multiplying 2 and 5 instead of isolating x.
~ x = 0 #The restriction says x = 0 is not allowed, and it would make the denominator zero.
= x = 2/5 #Correct. Multiply by x and divide by 5 to get x = 2/5, which is allowed.
}

::ALG2_U03_Pretest_Q08::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_Pretest_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.RR.5; MLA.A2.RR.6<br><br>Question:<br>For f(x) = 4/(x + 1), what is the vertical asymptote? {
~ x = 4 #The numerator does not create the vertical asymptote.
~ x = 1 #This reverses the sign of the denominator solution.
= x = -1 #Correct. Set x + 1 = 0, so the vertical asymptote is x = -1.
~ y = -1 #A vertical asymptote has the form x = a, not y = a.
}

::ALG2_U03_Pretest_Q09::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_Pretest_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.RR.5; MLA.A2.RR.6<br><br>Question:<br>A model T(n) = 80/n gives time in minutes when n workers share a job. Which input is not meaningful? {
~ n = 1 #One worker is meaningful in the context.
= n = 0 #Correct. Zero workers cannot complete the job, and division by zero is undefined.
~ n = 4 #Four workers is meaningful in the context.
~ n = 10 #Ten workers is meaningful in the context.
}

::ALG2_U03_Pretest_Q10::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_Pretest_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Unit 3 standards synthesis; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Why should solutions to radical equations be checked in the original equation? {
= Squaring can create extraneous solutions. #Correct. Squaring both sides can introduce candidates that do not satisfy the original radical equation.
~ Radical equations never have solutions. #Radical equations can have valid solutions when the original equation checks.
~ Checking changes the domain to all real numbers. #Checking verifies a candidate; it does not expand the domain.
~ Rational expressions are always undefined. #This confuses radical-equation checking with rational restrictions.
}
